This is a REAL potential source of intermittent data loss! Because `sifli_rxint(dev, true)` calls `up_enable_irq(uart->config->irq_type+16)`, and **uart_read() calls uart_enablerxint() on EVERY read() call** (serial.c line 1112/1287/1312) — i.e., the GNSS thread calls it every 200 ms. If a USART2 IRQ (IDLE flag / error) was pending in the NVIC at the moment `up_enable_irq` runs, the pending bit is *cleared*:

- The peripheral's IDLE flag remains set (it's in the UART's ISR register), BUT the NVIC pending bit is cleared. Would a new interrupt be latched? For level-triggered interrupts (which NVIC uses for peripherals), the NVIC re-pends the interrupt as long as the peripheral's interrupt line is asserted. But careful: for STM32-style NVIC, the "pending" bit for a level-sensitive IRQ is re-asserted on the next clock edge after it's cleared if the source line is still high. So clearing the pending bit while the source is still asserting would just delay it by a cycle — the interrupt would fire again immediately. Since the IDLE flag stays set until the ISR clears it (without the ISR running, the flag stays), the interrupt should re-trigger.

Hmm, unless the peripheral's interrupt line is a *pulse* (not level). For UART ISR flags, the interrupt line is level (OR of the enabled & set flags). So the pending-clear is mostly harmless — the interrupt will re-pend. BUT there's a catch: the NVIC "pending" mechanism for level-triggered lines: if the peripheral's line is asserted and then the software clears the pending bit, the hardware will set it again only on the next rising edge... For STM32 NVIC, an interrupt line that is level-high continuously results in the pending bit being set (it's a latch that gets set when the input goes high; when SW clears the pending bit while the line stays high, the bit is NOT automatically re-set until the input goes low and high again!).

Hmm, this is the classic "NVIC pending clear can lose an edge-triggered interrupt" concern. For STM32 (which uses level-sensitive interrupts from peripherals — actually the interrupt inputs are level-sensitive and the NVIC samples them each cycle), the pending bit will be set again on the next cycle if the line is still asserted. Cortex-M NVIC: "The NVIC intends that interrupt signals are level-sensitive... if the interrupt signal is still asserted, the pending bit is set again". Actually the ARM spec says: for level-triggered interrupts, if the signal remains asserted, the pending bit will be set again. Yes — ARM: "When the interrupt signal is asserted, the NVIC sets the pending bit... If software clears the pending bit while the signal remains asserted, the pending bit is set again." I believe for STM32 this is true (the NVIC samples the input line each clock). So the risk is limited to a 1-2 cycle delay. So the pending-clear in up_enable_irq is probably harmless but still a smell — and I should report it as "suspicious but likely benign" (it could delay/drop the IDLE ISR only in an edge-like scenario).

